  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x{e}^{2x}-x+3{x}^{2}}{{sin}^{2}\u2061x}=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x{e}^{2x}-x+3{x}^{2}}{{sin}^{2}\u2061x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[x{e}^{2x}-x+3{x}^{2}]^{\u2032}}{[{sin}^{2}\u2061x]^{\u2032}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(1+2x){e}^{2x}-1+6x}{2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(1+2x){e}^{2x}-1+6x}{\\operatorname{sin}\u20612x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x{e}^{2x}-x+3{x}^{2}}{{sin}^{2}\u2061x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(1+2x){e}^{2x}-1+6x}{\\operatorname{sin}\u20612x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[(1+2x){e}^{2x}-1+6x]^{\u2032}}{[\\operatorname{sin}\u20612x]^{\u2032}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(4+4x){e}^{2x}+6}{2\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(4+0){e}^{0}+6}{2\\operatorname{cos}\u20610} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+6}{2} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
